  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Sierra Nevada has $1.9B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date, across 38 awards and with an average award size of $49.5M. Obligations fell 16.2% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $1.9B, while the Department of Homeland Security shows $35K in obligations. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest market for Sierra Nevada, with $1.5B in obligations, followed by All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services at $297M and Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $114M. FY-by-FY obligations show growth into FY25 and a partial-year FY26 total of $1.9B: FY21 was $112M, FY22 was $539M, FY23 was $699M, FY24 was $1.3B, FY25 was $2.2B. The company’s portfolio for FY2026 to date concentrates on aircraft-related manufacturing and technical services, with smaller positions in computer systems design and telecommunications. Current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date), and the figures reported here are contract obligations except where noted otherwise in the awards tables.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What are the main drivers of Sierra Nevada’s FY2026 obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the primary driver, obligating $1.9B for FY2026 to date. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry driver with $1.5B in obligations, supported by $297M in professional, scientific, and technical services and $114M in search and detection systems manufacturing. Other smaller markets include Computer Systems Design Services at $4.7M and All Other Telecommunications at $3.8M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How did Sierra Nevada’s obligations change year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 16.2% year over year for the current fiscal-year-to-date comparison. Fiscal-year totals show FY25 at $2.2B and the partial FY26 total at $1.9B, after FY24 at $1.3B, FY23 at $699M, FY22 at $539M, and FY21 at $112M. The year-over-year change reflects those fiscal-year comparisons."
      }
    ]
  },
